[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that lets you keep the Latin labels coming from the
`glyphs` URL (Roboto Regular) **and** draw every Chinese character with a locally-hosted
**Noto Sans SC** font, without touching any other part of the style.

---

## 1.  What actually happens right now  

| Layer | `text-font` | `glyphs` URL | What GL JS does for CJK |
|------|-------------|--------------|------------------------|
| your label layers | `["Roboto Regular"]` | `https://…/font/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf` | The glyph request for the CJK range fails (Roboto has no CJK glyphs) → GL JS falls back to the **local-ideograph** path (`localIdeographFontFamily`, default = `sans-serif`). The browser therefore draws the characters with whatever generic sans-serif it finds on the client OS. |

Because the style **does have a `glyphs` field**, the “local-fonts-only” mode is *not* active – GL JS only uses the local-ideograph fallback for the CJK range.

---

## 2.  Two ways to force Noto Sans SC for CJK  

| Approach | Where the choice is made | Pros | Cons |
|----------|--------------------------|------|------|
| **A. `localIdeographFontFamily`** (map option) | Only for layers that keep the *default* `text-font` (i.e. no explicit `text-font` array). | Very simple – one line of JS. | Your layers *do* set `text-font` (`["Roboto Regular"]`), so this option **does not apply** to them. |
| **B. `font-faces` with a `unicode-range`** (style-level) | Works **exactly** for the `text-font` you already use (`Roboto Regular`). | Keeps the existing `text-font` array, works for any layer, works on both GL JS **and** Native. | Requires a small addition to the style (or a runtime call to `map.setFontFaces`). |

Because you already have `text-font: ["Roboto Regular"]`, **Option B** is the only one that will affect those layers.

---

## 3.  Prepare the Noto Sans SC file  

1. **Get the font** – e.g. `NotoSansSC-Regular.otf` from Google Fonts or the Noto repo.  
2. **Host it** on a domain that your map page can reach (same origin or CORS-enabled).  
   *Make sure the URL is reachable from the browser* (you can test with `fetch` in the console).  
3. **Optional but recommended:** add a CSS `@font-face` rule so the browser can use the font even if you later load it only via `font-faces`.  

```css
@font-face {
  font-family: "Noto Sans SC";
  src: url("https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf") format("opentype");
  font-weight: normal;
  font-style: normal;
}
```

---

## 4.  Add a `font-faces` entry that **only** covers CJK code points  

### 4.1  In the *style JSON* (static)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "glyphs": "https://my-glyphs.example.com/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "font-faces": {
    "Roboto Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf",
        "unicode-range": [
          "U+3000-30FF",   // CJK Symbols & Punctuation
          "U+4E00-9FFF",   // CJK Unified Ideographs
          "U+3400-4DBF",   // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension A
          "U+20000-2A6DF", // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension B
          "U+2A700-2B73F", // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension C
          "U+2B740-2B81F", // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension D
          "U+2B820-2CEAF", // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension E
          "U+2CEB0-2EBEF", // CJK Unified Ideographs Extension F
          "U+F900-FAFF",   // CJK Compatibility Ideographs
          "U+2F800-2FA1F", // CJK Compatibility Ideographs Supplement
          "U+FF00-FFEF"    // Half-width & Full-width Forms
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [
    /* … your layers … */
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["Roboto Regular"],   // unchanged
        "text-size": 12
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

**What this does**

* For every code point that falls inside one of the `unicode-range` blocks, GL JS will **download the font file** (`NotoSansSC-Regular.otf`) and render the glyph **directly from that file** (via the browser’s text engine).  
* For all other code points (Latin, Greek, Cyrillic, etc.) the normal glyph-PBF pipeline stays untouched – the glyphs are fetched from the `glyphs` URL and come from Roboto Regular.

### 4.2  Or add it *at runtime* (if you cannot edit the style file)

```javascript
// after creating the map, but before any layers that use the font are added
map.setFontFaces({
  "Roboto Regular": [
    {
      url: "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf",
      // same unicode-range as above
      "unicode-range": [
        "U+3000-30FF","U+4E00-9FFF","U+3400-4DBF",
        "U+20000-2A6DF","U+2A700-2B73F","U+2B740-2B81F",
        "U+2B820-2CEAF","U+2CEB0-2EBEF","U+F900-FAFF",
        "U+2F800-2FA1F","U+FF00-FFEF"
      ]
    }
  ]
});
```

> **Tip:** `map.setFontFaces` can be called **any time**; you can even swap the font later if you need a different CJK family.

---

## 5.  Verify it works  

1. Open the page, open DevTools → **Network** tab, filter by **font**.  
2. When you pan/zoom to an area that contains Chinese characters you should see a request like  

```
GET https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf
```

3. The **glyph** requests for the CJK range (`.../Roboto%20Regular/0-255.pbf`, etc.) should **not** appear – they are suppressed because the `unicode-range` tells GL JS to use the local font instead.  
4. The Latin labels still trigger the usual glyph-PBF requests (`.../Roboto%20Regular/0-255.pbf`, …) and look exactly as before.

---

## 6.  Edge cases & gotchas  

| Situation | What to watch for |
|-----------|-------------------|
| **CORS errors** | The font file must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your site’s origin). Otherwise the browser will block it and fall back to the generic sans-serif again. |
| **Cache** | Browsers cache the font file, so after the first successful load you won’t see further network requests. Clear the cache or use a cache-busting query string if you’re testing changes. |
| **Multiple `text-font` entries** | If you ever add a second fallback (e.g. `["Roboto Regular", "Noto Sans SC"]`) the `font-faces` entry **must** be attached to **each name** you want to use for CJK. In our case we keep a single name, so only one entry is needed. |
| **MapLibre Native** | The same `font-faces` block works on Android/iOS (since Native v11.13 / v6.18). Just make sure the URL points to a location the native app can reach (e.g. a CDN or bundled asset served via `file://` with a proper `url`). |
| **Future font-stack changes** | If you later switch the Latin font to something else (e.g. `Open Sans Regular`), keep the `font-faces` entry **under the new name** or add a second entry for the old name – otherwise CJK will revert to the generic sans-serif. |

---

## 7.  TL;DR – Minimal code snippet

```json
/* style.json – only the new part shown */
{
  "glyphs": "https://my-glyphs.example.com/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf",
  "font-faces": {
    "Roboto Regular": [
      {
        "url": "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf",
        "unicode-range": [
          "U+3000-30FF","U+4E00-9FFF","U+3400-4DBF",
          "U+20000-2A6DF","U+2A700-2B73F","U+2B740-2B81F",
          "U+2B820-2CEAF","U+2CEB0-2EBEF","U+F900-FAFF",
          "U+2F800-2FA1F","U+FF00-FFEF"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-font": ["Roboto Regular"],
        "text-size": 12
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

or, if you can’t edit the JSON:

```javascript
const map = new maplibregl.Map({ /* … */ });

map.setFontFaces({
  "Roboto Regular": [
    {
      url: "https://example.com/fonts/NotoSansSC-Regular.otf",
      "unicode-range": [
        "U+3000-30FF","U+4E00-9FFF","U+3400-4DBF",
        "U+20000-2A6DF","U+2A700-2B73F","U+2B740-2B81F",
        "U+2B820-2CEAF","U+2CEB0-2EBEF","U+F900-FAFF",
        "U+2F800-2FA1F","U+FF00-FFEF"
      ]
    }
  ]
});
```

That’s it – Chinese place names will now be rendered with **Noto Sans SC**, while all Latin text continues to use the Roboto glyphs you already serve. Happy mapping!
